  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/... \nStrengthening of the ridge is forecast through Friday before\ngradually weakening this weekend into Monday. Guidance agrees H5\nheights will rise to 596-598dm on Friday before dropping back to\n594-596dm by Sunday. The strength of this ridge will easily be \naround the 99th percentile of climatology across the region on \nFriday, dropping back to 90-97th percentile on Sunday. Due to the \nstrength of the ridge and some slight drying, temperatures are \nforecast to peak on Friday and/or Saturday with highs 110-115 \ndegrees. Surface dew points should fall back into the upper 50s to\nlower 60s during this time, but it will still feel somewhat humid\nas heat indices could reach as high as 115-120 degrees across the\nlower deserts of southeast California. There was enough \nconfidence in guidance to also extend the Extreme Heat Warning \nthrough Sunday as the convective potential should be fairly \nlimited. The strength of the high and slight dip in moisture is \nexpected keep CIN values quite high across the area, especially \nover the lower deserts. Higher terrain areas of eastern Arizona \nwill keep daily 15-30% chance PoPs, while the Phoenix area mostly\nstay between 10-15%. \n\nBy early next week, models show the ridge weakening again, while\nmost likely staying centered to our north. This should allow for \nbetter monsoon storm chances with models leaning toward some \nimproving moisture and better upper level support. NBM/WPC PoPs \nseem too high for Sunday at 30-40% over central and eastern \nArizona, but those chances may be more reasonable for Monday \nand/or Tuesday of next week. Temperatures will also drift lower \nearly next week due to the weakening of the ridge and with more \nconvective activity in the area. The latest NBM shows temperatures\nback into the normal range by around Monday or Tuesday.",
